WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:05.939
In the last lesson, we added an episodic
memory in the form of few-shot examples.

2
00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:09.075
In this lesson,
we're going to add in the third

3
00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:12.078
and final type of memory,
procedural memory.

4
00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:15.081
This will be in the form of the system
prompts

5
00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:18.151
that exist in the main agent
and also in the triage agent.

6
00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.821
So as a reminder, procedural memories

7
00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:24.824
are instructions about how to do things.

8
00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:27.193
And the representation that that takes in
agents

9
00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:30.196
is typically in the form
of prompt instructions.

10
00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:33.600
We've been using hardcoded values
for these throughout the lesson so far.

11
00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:35.535
In this lesson,

12
00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:38.772
we're going to make it possible
to update those prompts automatically.

13
00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:41.441
Let's dive in.

14
00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:43.910
The start should look pretty similar
to before.

15
00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.745
We're going to load some environment
variables.

16
00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:47.547
We're going to define our profile.

17
00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:50.283
And we're going to define
our prompt instructions here.

18
00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:53.153
This is what we're going
to be updating over time.

19
00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:56.122
So these are specific instructions
for when to ignore,

20
00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:59.592
when to notify, when to respond,
and how the overall agent should behave.

21
00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:03.196
And so these are hardcoded here
and have been hardcoded here.

22
00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:06.533
And we're still going to run this cell
to keep them here.

23
00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.202
But we're going to use them
in a slightly different way.

24
00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:10.470
And we'll dive into that later.

25
00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:14.574
This email is an

26
00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:18.445
example email. Here
we're going to load the in memory store

27
00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:21.481
the long-term memory store
which we're going to use for everything.

28
00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:25.819
We are going to define our helpers
for the triage step.

29
00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:28.221
And now we get down here.

30
00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:31.925
And here we can see
these are where our updated memories

31
00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.928
for the procedural steps are going to go.

32
00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:36.896
And so this is our this is our template.

33
00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:37.897
This is the same as before.

34
00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:40.900
But I'm just pointing out
that this is where they are going to go.

35
00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:46.106
We're going to define a bunch of stuff
for this triage step.

36
00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:47.907
We've got our LLM router.

37
00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:49.275
We've got our user prompt.

38
00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.910
We're defining the state of the graph.

39
00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.645
We're defining some things.

40
00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:54.981
And then this is our triage router node.

41
00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:57.684
And this is what we're eventually
going to modify.

42
00:01:57.684 --> 00:02:01.254
So right now, you can see that we're
passing in the prompt instructions here.

43
00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:05.758
What we're going to do
is we're going to modify it so that we are

44
00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:10.196
retrieving these from the long-term
memory store and then passing them in.

45
00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.832
Why are we doing this?

46
00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:17.504
We're going to do this so that we can
update them from outside the agent.

47
00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:21.441
And then that way if we update them,
they're automatically pulled in

48
00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:22.475
to future runs.

49
00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:29.983
Okay.

50
00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:32.285
So, how are we going to modify this.

51
00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:35.488
First, let's get our LangGraph user ID.

52
00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:40.059
And we're going to use this as
the namespace for the procedural memories.

53
00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:43.296
So we're getting our LangGraph
user ID from the config.

54
00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:46.299
And we're creating this namespace
which is a tuple.

55
00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:49.335
So make sure that you've got this
comma here.

56
00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:53.840
We're now going to try to fetch
from the store

57
00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.277
instructions for when to ignore
emails in the triage step.

58
00:02:57.544 --> 00:03:01.080
And so we're going to get from the store
with this given namespace

59
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.083
this key triage ignore.

60
00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:07.754
Now, this may not always
exist in the store.

61
00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:11.357
And so what we're going to do is
we're going to check to see if it exists.

62
00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:13.826
If it does
we're going to use the value there.

63
00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:16.829
If it doesn't,
we're going to add a default.

64
00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:20.533
This will make sure that
when we run this agent with a new user ID,

65
00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:24.170
even if we haven't already
set up the store, it will add the default

66
00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:24.437
there.

67
00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:28.341
So here
we have the logic if the result is none.

68
00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:32.779
So if nothing exists in the store
for these ignore triage instructions,

69
00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:34.180
we are going to put something there.

70
00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.683
We're going to put something
in that namespace with that key.

71
00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:39.686
And it's going to be a dictionary
with one key prompt.

72
00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:41.054
And the values are going to be

73
00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:44.057
those default prompt instructions
that we had for ignore.

74
00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:46.993
And then are we're going to set
ignore prompt,

75
00:03:46.993 --> 00:03:50.897
which we're going to use later
on to that default value.

76
00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:54.567
If the result does exist
we're going to get the value from prompt.

77
00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.269
And that's what we're going to use.

78
00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:59.272
And so then we're going to pass it in

79
00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:00.273
down here.

80
00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:03.276
So let's update this to be ignore prompt.

81
00:04:05.478 --> 00:04:08.014
We now want to do the same for notify.

82
00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:09.282
So we are going to now

83
00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:12.885
use the same namespace
but with a different key, triage notify.

84
00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:15.488
And we're going to get notify prompt.

85
00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:20.093
Similarly, we're going to update the
formatting down below to use this value.

86
00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:24.831
And finally we're going to do the same
for the respond prompt.

87
00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:26.332
So now it's triage

88
00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:27.867
respond is the key.

89
00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:30.503
And respond
prompt is the new variable we get.

90
00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:33.506
And we're going to insert that here.

91
00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:39.846
So we've just updated our agent
to use prompts that are stored

92
00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:41.281
in long-term memory.

93
00:04:41.281 --> 00:04:43.716
Note that there's nothing
that updates them yet.

94
00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:45.618
We're going to go over that later on.

95
00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:48.621
But this just updated

96
00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:50.390
this triage router

97
00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:53.393
to use stuff in long-term memory.

98
00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:55.995
And this is step number one.

99
00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:58.564
After that we're going to go on
to the rest of the agent.

100
00:04:58.564 --> 00:04:59.999
So we're going to import the tool.

101
00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:02.702
We're going to create the three
different tools we have.

102
00:05:02.702 --> 00:05:06.406
We are going to import the memory tools
and use those.

103
00:05:06.773 --> 00:05:09.909
We are going to have
the system message for the agent.

104
00:05:10.343 --> 00:05:13.313
And now we get down to create prompt.

105
00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:16.416
So, create prompt is where we are
also going to update.

106
00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:19.852
So right now, we're passing in the prompt
instructions with agent instructions.

107
00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:22.488
And we're going to do the same thing
as above.

108
00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:23.356
We're going to update it.

109
00:05:23.356 --> 00:05:26.359
So it looks for these in the long-term
memory store

110
00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:27.193
first.

111
00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:37.870
All right.

112
00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.039
So now we've modified it in a few ways.

113
00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:42.675
We've passed in the config. And the store.

114
00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:45.511
We're going to use the config
to get the LangGraph user ID.

115
00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:48.414
And that's going to create the namespace
that we look in.

116
00:05:48.414 --> 00:05:51.284
The key that we're going to use
is agent instructions.

117
00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:53.720
And we're going to do similar logic
as before.

118
00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:56.923
We're going to first
try to get this result from the store.

119
00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:58.524
If it does not exist,

120
00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:02.161
we're going to put a default one in there
using prompt instructions as the default.

121
00:06:02.495 --> 00:06:05.498
If it does, we're actually
just going to use the value that's there.

122
00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:08.534
And then what we're going to do, is
we're going to update the system message.

123
00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:11.404
And rather than using
the prompt instructions here,

124
00:06:12.772 --> 00:06:13.373
we are going to

125
00:06:13.373 --> 00:06:16.342
use the prompt value
that we get from above.

126
00:06:17.844 --> 00:06:20.847
With that now done
we can create our main agent.

127
00:06:20.947 --> 00:06:22.815
So we can import create react agent.

128
00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:26.419
We define a bunch of tools
and then we create the email agent.

129
00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:30.022
So now this agent is all set up.

130
00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:33.292
It's pulling its instructions
from long-term memory

131
00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:36.262
rather than from those hardcoded values.

132
00:06:36.396 --> 00:06:37.764
Why does this matter?

133
00:06:37.764 --> 00:06:40.500
This matters
because now we can update those values

134
00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:44.704
outside the agent and automatically update
the agent's instructions.

135
00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:48.741
So now let's see how to do that.

136
00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:51.677
Let's work with this example email.

137
00:06:51.677 --> 00:06:53.112
Hi John, urgent issue.

138
00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:55.681
Your service is down.

139
00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:57.650
Let's define
the config that we're going to use.

140
00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.520
In this case
it has the LangGraph user id of Lance.

141
00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:06.159
Let's
now run this email agent over this email.

142
00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:09.162
And we can see that we get a response.

143
00:07:11.564 --> 00:07:15.168
If we look through the messages
we can see that we have a human message.

144
00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:19.338
Then the AI message
that is calling the write email tool

145
00:07:19.338 --> 00:07:20.940
and it's writing this email.

146
00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:24.277
And then the response from the tool
message saying that we sent this email.

147
00:07:24.277 --> 00:07:27.280
And then a final response from the agent.

148
00:07:27.346 --> 00:07:30.349
Let's take a look
at the existing long-term memory

149
00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:33.352
for these instructions.

150
00:07:33.519 --> 00:07:36.722
If we look at the lance namespace
with the key

151
00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:40.860
agent instructions,
we can see these default instructions.

152
00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:44.363
These are the same as where in the prompt
instructions at the top of the notebook.

153
00:07:46.165 --> 00:07:50.002
Let's look and we should see that
the triage respond is also populated.

154
00:07:52.505 --> 00:07:53.539
As is triage

155
00:07:53.539 --> 00:07:56.542
ignore.

156
00:07:57.076 --> 00:08:00.079
And as is triage notify.

157
00:08:00.413 --> 00:08:03.649
If we wanted to,
we could update these instructions

158
00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:06.652
manually and pass in new instructions.

159
00:08:07.086 --> 00:08:08.354
But that's kind of a manual update.

160
00:08:08.354 --> 00:08:10.890
That's not really memory in terms
of how we think about it

161
00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:14.393
in terms of agents. With agents,
we think about in

162
00:08:14.393 --> 00:08:18.197
LLM updating
these based on some user feedback.

163
00:08:18.831 --> 00:08:21.834
So that's exactly what we're going to do.

164
00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:24.036
So we're going to import from Langmem

165
00:08:24.036 --> 00:08:27.139
this function called create
multi prompt optimizer.

166
00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:30.510
As the name suggests,
this is going to create

167
00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:33.713
an optimizer
that optimizes multiple prompts.

168
00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.284
The reason we want to optimize
multiple prompts is that in this assistant

169
00:08:38.651 --> 00:08:41.654
there are actually four different
prompt instructions that we have.

170
00:08:41.821 --> 00:08:44.023
And we might want
to update any number of them,

171
00:08:45.091 --> 00:08:48.127
but we
don't get feedback for each instruction.

172
00:08:48.394 --> 00:08:51.497
Rather, we get feedback
for the whole overall agent.

173
00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:55.535
So we're going to take this overall
feedback

174
00:08:55.535 --> 00:08:58.804
and first determine which prompts,
if any, should be updated

175
00:08:58.804 --> 00:09:01.807
based on this feedback
and then update them.

176
00:09:03.075 --> 00:09:04.443
First, we need a list

177
00:09:04.443 --> 00:09:07.446
of the conversations that we're going
to use to update the prompts.

178
00:09:08.147 --> 00:09:10.716
For this,
we're going to take the response messages.

179
00:09:10.716 --> 00:09:13.553
So this is the trajectory
that we have above.

180
00:09:13.553 --> 00:09:15.922
And then we're going to add
in some feedback.

181
00:09:15.922 --> 00:09:19.792
This can be in a few different formats
but the simplest format is just a string.

182
00:09:20.226 --> 00:09:21.694
So here
we're going to take in this feedback.

183
00:09:21.694 --> 00:09:24.697
Always sign your emails John Doe.

184
00:09:27.366 --> 00:09:28.634
We now need to define

185
00:09:28.634 --> 00:09:31.737
the prompts
that we want to potentially update.

186
00:09:33.306 --> 00:09:36.309
Each entry
in this list should have four keys.

187
00:09:36.375 --> 00:09:38.611
First, is the name of the prompt.

188
00:09:38.611 --> 00:09:41.314
Next, is the value of the prompt.

189
00:09:41.314 --> 00:09:42.882
Next, is update instructions.

190
00:09:42.882 --> 00:09:45.785
These are instructions
for how you should update the prompt.

191
00:09:45.785 --> 00:09:48.788
And then finally, we have the
when to update key.

192
00:09:49.055 --> 00:09:51.991
These are instructions
for when you should update the prompt.

193
00:09:51.991 --> 00:09:55.328
So under the hood what's going to happen
is we're first going to use the

194
00:09:55.328 --> 00:09:59.932
when to update value to determine
which prompts, if any, to update.

195
00:10:00.433 --> 00:10:03.436
If we determined that it's worth updating,
we're then going to use

196
00:10:03.502 --> 00:10:07.173
the update instructions
and apply that to the original prompt

197
00:10:07.373 --> 00:10:10.209
to get a new updated prompt.

198
00:10:10.209 --> 00:10:12.612
Here, we've filled these in
for the four prompts

199
00:10:12.612 --> 00:10:14.547
that we want to potentially update.

200
00:10:14.547 --> 00:10:16.349
So we have the main agent prompt,

201
00:10:16.349 --> 00:10:19.585
which we're going to get
the most current values from this agent

202
00:10:19.585 --> 00:10:21.287
instructions key.

203
00:10:22.488 --> 00:10:22.955
The update

204
00:10:22.955 --> 00:10:26.125
instructions are just going to be keep
the instructions short and to the point.

205
00:10:26.125 --> 00:10:28.594
And we're actually going to use that
for all of them.

206
00:10:28.594 --> 00:10:30.696
And then when to update. Updates prompt

207
00:10:30.696 --> 00:10:34.133
whenever there is feedback on how
the agent should write emails or schedule

208
00:10:34.133 --> 00:10:36.369
events.

209
00:10:36.369 --> 00:10:38.604
What about triage ignore?

210
00:10:38.604 --> 00:10:40.539
Similarly, we're going to name it that.

211
00:10:40.539 --> 00:10:43.275
We're going to fetch the most recent value
from long-term memory.

212
00:10:43.275 --> 00:10:45.144
Update instructions are the same.

213
00:10:45.144 --> 00:10:46.345
And then update this prompt

214
00:10:46.345 --> 00:10:49.315
whenever there's feedback
on which emails should be ignored.

215
00:10:50.282 --> 00:10:52.985
Triaged notify and triage
respond are pretty similar,

216
00:10:52.985 --> 00:10:54.854
except rather than updating it

217
00:10:54.854 --> 00:10:57.857
when there is information
about when the email should be ignored,

218
00:10:57.890 --> 00:10:59.659
we're going to update it
when there's information

219
00:10:59.659 --> 00:11:02.428
about when the user
should be notified or responded.

220
00:11:03.562 --> 00:11:04.230
After we do

221
00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.233
this, we're going to create the Multi
prompt optimizer.

222
00:11:07.266 --> 00:11:11.470
We're going to use Anthropic's Claude
three sonnet model to optimize the prompt.

223
00:11:11.504 --> 00:11:15.241
We've found that it's currently the best
for this type of prompt optimization.

224
00:11:16.542 --> 00:11:19.178
Kind equals prompt memory is the algorithm

225
00:11:19.178 --> 00:11:21.514
that we're going to use
to update the prompt.

226
00:11:21.514 --> 00:11:23.315
This is the simplest one.

227
00:11:23.315 --> 00:11:24.850
And you should look at the API reference

228
00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.853
of Langmem for more complicated
examples.

229
00:11:28.287 --> 00:11:31.290
This optimizer itself is a graph.

230
00:11:31.524 --> 00:11:35.127
So we're going to invoke it
by passing in these trajectories,

231
00:11:35.127 --> 00:11:36.662
and then these prompts.

232
00:11:36.662 --> 00:11:39.665
And we're going to get back
this updated variable.

233
00:11:40.232 --> 00:11:43.235
Let's take a look at what this returns.

234
00:11:43.335 --> 00:11:47.073
We can see that
it returns a list of prompts.

235
00:11:47.807 --> 00:11:50.076
These have all the same keys as before.

236
00:11:50.076 --> 00:11:53.512
So the name prompt update instructions
and when to update.

237
00:11:54.246 --> 00:11:57.383
Now some of these prompts
may be different than before.

238
00:11:57.383 --> 00:11:59.318
And we can actually see that here.

239
00:11:59.318 --> 00:12:00.953
The first main agent prompt.

240
00:12:00.953 --> 00:12:02.455
It's prompt is now different.

241
00:12:03.823 --> 00:12:06.826
When sending
emails always sign them as John Doe.

242
00:12:06.892 --> 00:12:09.862
This is the feedback that we passed in.

243
00:12:09.962 --> 00:12:12.898
Once we have these updated prompts,

244
00:12:12.898 --> 00:12:16.936
we can loop through them and update
the long term memory if appropriate.

245
00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:21.440
So here you can see that we're comparing
the updated prompt to the old prompt.

246
00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:24.143
And we're comparing the prompt values.

247
00:12:24.143 --> 00:12:25.578
If they're not the same,

248
00:12:25.578 --> 00:12:28.581
we're going to update the store
with their instructions.

249
00:12:28.748 --> 00:12:31.484
Here, I've only added logic to handle
what would have happened

250
00:12:31.484 --> 00:12:34.787
if the name of the prompt
that's been updated is Main Agent.

251
00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:38.324
But remember, there's three other prompts
that could potentially be updated.

252
00:12:38.724 --> 00:12:41.527
They shouldn't,
so they shouldn't throw an error.

253
00:12:41.527 --> 00:12:45.698
But for completeness, you'll probably
want to implement all three conditions.

254
00:12:47.199 --> 00:12:50.603
And we can see that we print out updated
main agent prompt as we went along.

255
00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:53.973
And we didn't raise any value errors,
so no other prompts got updated.

256
00:12:57.777 --> 00:12:59.078
If we now check the long-term

257
00:12:59.078 --> 00:13:02.915
memory for these agent instructions,
we can see that it now has this sentence,

258
00:13:02.915 --> 00:13:05.918
when sending emails,
always sign them as John Doe.

259
00:13:06.685 --> 00:13:09.388
We can now rerun the email agent

260
00:13:09.388 --> 00:13:12.258
and see what happens.

261
00:13:12.258 --> 00:13:14.660
If we take a look at the messages,

262
00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:18.898
we can see that when we write the email
we now sign it John Doe.

263
00:13:19.031 --> 00:13:22.067
So it's remembering
and using these instructions.

264
00:13:23.736 --> 00:13:26.539
Let's look at another example.

265
00:13:26.539 --> 00:13:28.007
We now have this email.

266
00:13:28.007 --> 00:13:29.842
And we invoke the email agent.

267
00:13:29.842 --> 00:13:32.845
And it responds to this email above.

268
00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:37.183
What if we didn't
want it to respond to this?

269
00:13:37.683 --> 00:13:39.919
We could
then create a list of conversations

270
00:13:39.919 --> 00:13:42.655
with the response messages
as the conversation,

271
00:13:42.655 --> 00:13:44.056
and we could give the feedback:

272
00:13:44.056 --> 00:13:46.692
Ignore any emails from Alice Jones.

273
00:13:48.427 --> 00:13:50.596
We then define the list of prompts,

274
00:13:50.596 --> 00:13:51.530
and we're running this again,

275
00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:53.299
because the old list of prompts
that we defined

276
00:13:53.299 --> 00:13:56.669
before has the outdated instructions
for the main agent.

277
00:13:57.102 --> 00:13:59.939
And so we're just running it again
to get the most updated instructions

278
00:13:59.939 --> 00:14:02.942
for the agent at this point in time.

279
00:14:03.075 --> 00:14:05.811
After that,
we can invoke this optimizer agent

280
00:14:05.811 --> 00:14:08.814
with the conversations
we have above and the prompts.

281
00:14:10.316 --> 00:14:14.253
We can now run this code snippet
to update the prompts in long-term memory.

282
00:14:14.687 --> 00:14:18.991
Notice that we've added this new section
which handles how to deal with the fact,

283
00:14:19.358 --> 00:14:22.895
if the updated name of the prompt
that we're updating is the ignore step.

284
00:14:23.963 --> 00:14:26.999
And here we can see that
this was the only prompt of the four

285
00:14:27.299 --> 00:14:28.500
that we decided to update.

286
00:14:31.737 --> 00:14:32.271
And if we

287
00:14:32.271 --> 00:14:35.307
rerun the email agent from here,
we can see that it actually

288
00:14:35.307 --> 00:14:39.011
now ignores it, because it's
using the same email from Alice Jones.

289
00:14:39.278 --> 00:14:42.314
And it now has the instructions
to ignore emails from there.

290
00:14:45.384 --> 00:14:47.720
To see what exactly
these instructions are,

291
00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:51.957
we can look into the long-term
memory itself, in the triage ignore key,

292
00:14:51.957 --> 00:14:55.194
and we can see that
this is now updated to contain ignore

293
00:14:55.194 --> 00:14:58.197
all emails from Alice Jones.

294
00:14:59.365 --> 00:15:02.167
This is the end of this lesson.

295
00:15:02.167 --> 00:15:04.370
This puts together all the types of memory

296
00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:07.373
into this email agent.

297
00:15:07.539 --> 00:15:09.375
Now is a good time to try this out.

298
00:15:09.375 --> 00:15:13.412
Try passing in other conversations
with other feedback and see

299
00:15:13.612 --> 00:15:17.583
which parts of memory get updated
and what they get updated to.

